title:   Sprites: from the sheet to the screen
level:   the image editor and the assembler
needs:   assembler, emulator, rom
learn:   what File ▸ Export Bitplanes writes, and how a masked blit reads it
source:  repo demo/spritedemo/sprites.s

step  Assemble the demo
  teach  This one already exists in the tree: demo/spritedemo. It is a whole
         bitplane pipeline — the palette, a pre-shifted masked blit, six frames of
         animation and a Vsync to hold them apart. It assembles the same way
         anything else does, which is the point: nothing about it is special.
  do     build
  expect problems == none

step  Run it
  teach  {key:run} boots it and stops at the entry point. From there Continue is
         the program's own loop: a sprite moving through the frames, held in step
         with the screen by Vsync.
  do     run
  expect stopped

step  What is actually in the .dat
  teach  File ▸ Export Bitplanes wrote sprites.dat beside the source, and the
         listing pulls it in with `incbin`. A 16-pixel group in that data is a
         mask word followed by four plane words: the blit ANDs the mask into the
         screen and ORs each plane over it, so a set mask bit keeps whatever was
         underneath. That is why a sprite can be drawn over a background instead
         of punching a hole in it. The export dialog's file map lists every
         block's label and offset, and the `equ`s at the end of the listing are
         that map, trimmed to what the program uses.
  do     disasm
  nominal the data layout is the lesson here, and the listing is the evidence
